Pike 9.1: Changes since Pike 9.0
----------------------------------------------------------------------

New language features
---------------------

o Syntax for default values in multi-assignment.

  It is now possible to specify default values in multi-
  assignment.

    [int a, int b = a + 4] = ({ 1 });

  or

    [int a, int b = a + 4] = ({ 1, UNDEFINED });

  Both of the above statements will assign the value 1 to
  the variable a, and the value 5 to the variable b.

o Rest variables in multi-assignment.

  The final variable can collect the remaining array elements:

    [mixed head, mixed ... tail] = values;

  Existing array variables can be used with [... tail]. The rest array
  is empty if there are no remaining elements. Defaults and rest variables
  can also be used in function argument patterns.

  Unnamed elements discard individual positions, and a bare trailing ...
  discards the remaining elements:

    [mixed a, mixed, mixed c, ...] = ({ 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 });

  This assigns 0 to a and 2 to c.

o Mapping patterns in multi-assignment.

  Keys can select the values to assign from a mapping:

    ["year": int year, "mon": int mon, "mday": int mday] = localtime(time());

  Patterns can contain existing lvalues, declarations, defaults, and nested
  patterns. Missing keys supply UNDEFINED; other keys are ignored. The
  expression returns the source mapping. Mapping patterns can also be used
  as function arguments.


Bug fixes
---------

o Compiler

  - Integer addition and += preserve UNDEFINED when all operands are
    undefined, including optimized integer regions and C opcode fast paths.

  - Correct argument types and values for functions using multi-assignment
    with default values. Pattern locals no longer occupy incoming argument
    slots when a default expression captures them.

  - Inheritance can now delay a derived module's final compilation until an
    inherited program's compilation unit has finished. This avoids some
    loading-order-dependent failures when modules refer to each other's
    types. The base must already have completed its first pass; unresolved
    placeholders and cyclic final-pass dependencies are still rejected.
    Pike 9.0 and earlier compatibility modes retain the old restrictions.

  - Keep mutable container construction visible to the normal optimizer.
    This restores array append optimizations and avoids replacing simple
    array allocation with expensive recursive copies of constants.

o Runtime

o Operator functions


Incompatible changes
--------------------


New modules and functions
-------------------------

o Debug.get_program_layout() accepts an optional flag to include the code
  buffer base for sampling profilers. Undefined function declarations no
  longer produce invalid addresses in its function map.


New features
------------

o Compiler

  - SSA-enabled builds now enable integer-region optimizations by default
    on AMD64 and SPARC64 with 64-bit Pike integers. Set the environment
    variable PIKE_SSA_NO_INTEGER_REGIONS=1 to disable them.

  - SPARC and SPARC64 now emit fixed-arity builtin calls when the argument
    count is known, independently of SSA. Calls with dynamic argument counts
    retain mark-stack handling.

  - SPARC32 now also supports integer regions with 32-bit Pike integers.

  - Integer regions now track retained local-assignment results on a virtual
    Pike stack by default. Overflow exits publish the completed local and
    stack state before resuming the failed operation. Set the environment
    variable PIKE_SSA_NO_INTEGER_STACK=1 to select
    the earlier balanced-unit planner for comparisons.

  - Successful integer-region exits can also publish live stack results for
    subsequent ordinary opcodes. Set PIKE_SSA_NO_INTEGER_STACK_EXITS=1 to
    require successful exits to return to their entry stack depth.

  - Integer regions now lower variable and immediate subtraction with native
    signed overflow checks. Set PIKE_SSA_NO_INTEGER_SUBTRACT=1 to restore the
    previous handling for comparisons.

  - Integer regions also keep AND, OR, and XOR operations in native value
    banks, including masks mixed with arithmetic. Logical steps omit
    overflow checks while preserving neighboring arithmetic side exits.
    Set PIKE_SSA_NO_INTEGER_BITWISE=1 for the previous region handling.

  - Integer regions can now span adjacent source lines, preserving source
    positions for fallback calls and exceptions. File changes, debug
    directives and labels remain region boundaries.

  - SSA integer regions now lower unary negation and bounded multi-operand
    builtin addition on AMD64, SPARC32 and SPARC64. Overflow replays each
    original operation with its operands intact. Set
    PIKE_SSA_NO_INTEGER_EXPRESSIONS=1 to restore previous handling.

  - Integer regions can now retain constant-local assignments, including
    zero clears, on AMD64, SPARC32 and SPARC64. Integer guards preserve
    reference-release callbacks on other types; overflow exits publish
    completed constant assignments before replay. Set
    PIKE_SSA_NO_INTEGER_CONSTANT_ASSIGN=1 to restore previous planning.

  - SSA integer regions can consume one guarded integer already on the
    evaluation stack, allowing more expressions involving global values to
    use native arithmetic. Set PIKE_SSA_NO_INTEGER_STACK_INPUT=1 to restore
    previous planning.

  - SSA integer regions on AMD64, SPARC32 and SPARC64 now read a guarded
    direct global integer into a value register without first pushing it
    on the evaluation stack. Other contexts and types retain their existing
    indexing semantics and incoming-stack fast path, reusing the initial
    program check. Set PIKE_SSA_NO_INTEGER_GLOBAL_INPUT=1 to restore
    previous planning.

  - SSA integer regions can now commit their final result directly to the
    same guarded global on AMD64, SPARC32 and SPARC64. Popped assignment
    results avoid the evaluation stack. Overflow, indirect setters and
    noninteger values retain ordinary replay. Set
    PIKE_SSA_NO_INTEGER_GLOBAL_STORE=1 to restore previous store handling.

  - SSA-enabled builds optimize an isolated local/local addition followed
    by assignment/pop on AMD64, SPARC32 and SPARC64 by default. Set
    PIKE_SSA_NO_INTEGER_SINGLE_UPDATE=1 to disable this optimization.
    Assignments to either input are supported without reordering operands
    on type-guard or overflow replay. Other short regions retain their
    existing profitability limits.
    SPARC balanced integer regions also avoid redundant pointer reloads
    and empty overflow branches to reduce generated code size.

  - Set PIKE_SSA_INTEGER_SINGLE_ASSIGN=1 to experiment with isolated
    addition into a separate local (c=a+b or c=a+a) in SSA-enabled builds on
    AMD64, SPARC32 and SPARC64. The old destination needs no numeric load;
    reference values retain ordinary assignment and release through replay.
    SPARC combines entry guards, fills branch delay slots and avoids redundant
    pointer reloads through entry and ordinary replay to reduce generated code.

  - Set PIKE_SSA_INTEGER_PROFILE=1 to collect per-region execution counts,
    type-guard failures and overflow exits. Summarize captured stderr with
    tools/ssa_integer_profile.pike; ordinary runs emit no counter code.
    Profiles also report rejected candidates and planner stop reasons. Use
    --boundaries to rank them by region and rejected-candidate entry counts.


